Compiler for WACC, a Java-like language with functions, structs (including
inheritance), arrays, pairs, and the usual control flow. It type-checks a
program and emits ARM11 assembly.

Heap-allocated pairs, arrays, and structs are reclaimed by a mark-and-sweep
garbage collector in gc/. The collector walks the object graph from stack roots,
marks reachable objects, and frees the rest.

----------------------------
Files and directories
----------------------------

> antlr <

The antlr directory contains the ANTLR lexer and parser specifications
(WACCLexer.g4 and WACCParser.g4), along with a script antlrBuild that generates
the corresponding Java class files using the ANTLR libraries (more details
below).

> lib <

The lib directory contains the ANTLR library (antlr-4.5.3-complete.jar).
You should not need to make any changes in this directory.

> src <

The Scala compiler: parsing, semantic analysis, and ARM code generation.

> gc <

Mark-and-sweep garbage collector (C++). Built as ARM objects and linked with
compiled programs. See gc/gc.cpp for the mark/sweep pass and gc/objects.hpp
for the heap object types.

> grun <

The grun script runs the ANTLR TestRig, which is useful for debugging the lexer
and parser (more details below).

> compile <

Frontend script for the compiler. Pass a .wacc file to compile it to ARM
assembly, or --interactive for a simple REPL.

> Makefile <

Running 'make' in the root directory builds the compiler: it runs antlrBuild,
compiles generated Java, cross-compiles the GC, and packages the Scala sources
with sbt.

----------------------------
Using the provided scripts
----------------------------

> antlrBuild <

This script takes the ANTLR lexer and parser files (set within the script) and
creates the corresponding Java classes. The Java files are written to
src/main/java/antlr and should not be modified by hand. By default this script
generates a parse tree and a visitor for traversing it; you can change the
options in the script if you want different outputs.

Important! - running the antlrBuild script will overwrite the antlr directory
             under src. Do not write any of your code in src/main/java/antlr.

> grun <

This script is a wrapper around ANTLR's TestRig in the project environment. It
is already set up for the WACC grammar, starting from the 'program' rule. You
still choose the kind of output you want.

For example:
  ./grun -tokens
will run the TestRig and print the tokens seen by the lexer. To see how the
parser groups these tokens you can use -tree or -gui instead:
  ./grun -gui
In either case you will need to type in your input program and then close the
input stream with ctrl-D.

Rather than typing programs in by hand, you can pass a file through stdin:
  ./grun -gui < testfile
When using the TestRig this way you won't need to hit ctrl-D; the EOF in the
file closes the input stream for you.

> compile <

Compiles a WACC source file to ARM11 assembly in the current directory
(foo.wacc -> foo.s), or starts the interactive frontend:

  ./compile path/to/program.wacc
  ./compile --interactive

The ANTLR jar is already on the classpath in this script.

> emulate.sh <

Takes as an argument the path to an ARM11 program
Creates an executable with a cross-compiler for ARM11
Emulates the created executable (links in the GC)

> execute.sh <

Takes as an argument the path to a WACC program
Compiles the WACC file to ARM11 assembly
Creates an executable with a cross-compiler for ARM11
Emulates the created executable

> test_exitcodes.sh <
Compiles the tests in "wacc_examples", checking the compiler exit code against
the expected one. "make" has to be run first.
